00:01:49 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you, and you're trying to think it through using what seems like straightforward logic. It's not a crazy theory, and I can see why the connection feels obvious. But the missing piece—the reason that logic doesn't hold—is something that's often misunderstood, so let me address it directly. The idea that a gay man's attraction to adult men "naturally puts that attraction closer to kids" assumes that sexual orientation exists on a smooth spectrum that runs from child to adult, so that being attracted to adult men means a partial step toward being attracted to male children. That's simply not how human sexuality works. The clinical and biological reality is that sexual attraction to adults—whether heterosexual or homosexual—is a fundamentally different orientation from the paraphilic disorder of pedophilia. They aren't points on the same scale, they're distinct categories. A gay man is attracted to adult male physical characteristics that boys do not possess, just as a straight man is attracted to adult female body traits that girls do not have. Being gay doesn't mean "attracted to males in general" any more than being straight means "attracted to females in general." Adult orientation is specifically about sexually mature bodies and adult relationship dynamics, not a vague pull toward any male regardless of age. If your "common sense biology" theory were correct, we would see the same logic applied consistently: straight men would be considered a heightened risk to underage girls because their attraction to adult women "naturally puts that attraction closer to kids." But we don't treat that as a reason to exclude straight men from teaching or coaching girls, because we inherently understand that normal heterosexual attraction doesn't bleed into attraction to children. The same is true for gay men and boys. The leap from "attracted to men" to "therefore a risk to boys" is a non sequitur, not a biological fact. So I'm not dismissing you